1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,172
When people share a home, nobody owns the shopping.

2
00:00:06,172 --> 00:00:12,331
Things run out, get bought twice, and every group cost depends on a head count nobody can pin down.

3
00:00:12,331 --> 00:00:15,814
Stocked is a shopping agent for people who share a home.

4
00:00:15,814 --> 00:00:18,365
Nobody tracks anything, and nobody chats.

5
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:23,341
This is the house agent, four flatmates, and the agents and systems around them.

6
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:26,299
It starts with context it can trust.

7
00:00:26,299 --> 00:00:30,177
Airbyte syncs the flat's receipts and everyone's calendars.

8
00:00:30,177 --> 00:00:36,881
A small Superlinked model reads each receipt line, and a line only counts if it appears word for word.

9
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:42,927
Run-out dates are computed in SQL on SingleStore, from how often the flat re-buys each item.

10
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:45,697
Every decision shows its evidence.

11
00:00:45,697 --> 00:00:53,332
Here is why coffee is marked as out: the receipts it read, the exact lines it matched, and the flatmate who reported it.

12
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:56,784
A model proposes a basket, but code decides.

13
00:00:56,784 --> 00:01:00,347
Small restocks are ordered automatically, in test mode.

14
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:09,352
This party plan was over the house limit, so it waited for Maya, and Persona confirmed a real person was there before her approval counted.

15
00:01:09,352 --> 00:01:14,599
A request for a bluetooth speaker was refused, because it is not a shared supply.

16
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,462
Group plans follow the head count.

17
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:22,184
Each person's agent answers from their own calendar and spending limit.

18
00:01:22,184 --> 00:01:24,446
Leo is away, so his agent said no.

19
00:01:24,446 --> 00:01:27,838
For three people, transit keeps everyone in budget.

20
00:01:27,838 --> 00:01:29,634
For four, a cab is cheaper.

21
00:01:29,634 --> 00:01:38,281
Every plan has a backup, and if the venue's page shows a closure, the agent switches only when that costs no more than was agreed.

22
00:01:38,867 --> 00:01:41,274
We tested all of this with Firedrill.

23
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:48,626
Simulated flatmates talk to the agent, and payments go to a synthetic Stripe, so charges are counted, not judged.

24
00:01:48,626 --> 00:01:54,482
Round zero found a real bug: after a payment outage, a small order could never be retried.

25
00:01:54,482 --> 00:01:58,450
We fixed it, and the same scenario then charged exactly once.

26
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:05,152
Round two turned on backup plans, and a closed restaurant now ends with one refund and no extra charge.

27
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:10,055
Everything the agent does lands in one audit trail in SingleStore.

28
00:02:10,055 --> 00:02:20,256
Each conversation, with its tool calls and scores, goes to Amplitude, tagged with the agent version, and Statsig gates decide which behaviour is switched on.

29
00:02:20,867 --> 00:02:21,396
Stocked.

30
00:02:21,396 --> 00:02:25,366
Reliable context, a real action, and the evidence behind it.

31
00:02:25,366 --> 00:02:30,527
Every payment here is test mode, and the number of real-world actions is zero.
